<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plovdiv</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Philippopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plovdiv is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> river in the Upper Thracian Plain. Inhabited since prehistoric times, it was the Thracian Eumolpias, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>'s Philippopolis and the Roman capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, with a well-preserved Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. From 1878 to 1885 it was the capital of Eastern Rumelia, and the coup there in September 1885 brought union with Bulgaria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plovdiv.jpg" alt="Plovdiv" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Община Пловдив · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%B2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
